Language: en

00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:15.450 align:start position:0%
 
you  I am Eugenie Scott I'm the executive  director of the National Center for  Science Education we are in Oakland  California where a membership  organization most of our members are  scientists teachers people concerned  about church and state separation we  monitor the creationism and evolution  controversy and we try to help teachers  and school boards and legislators and  citizens to cope with pressures against  the teaching of evolution so in the 25  years or so that we've been doing this  we have found that there are three  arguments that pretty much any  creationist contention can be put into  if it's a video or a letter to the  editor our comment on a talk show  whatever it'll pretty much fall into one  of these three categories and we call  them the pillars of creationism I'd like  to tell you a little bit about the  pillars and why they're important to  recognize because you as a scientist or  a citizen who enjoy science who is  interested in science I hope is  concerned about what's going on in  American public schools and the science  classes there because at ncsc we hear a  lot about pressures from school boards  upon curriculum we hear pressures upon  individual teachers from parents we hear  about pressures from school boards or  state legislators that trickle down to  the classroom teacher as well and in all  of these cases there's there's been this  continuing story for over 30 years of  people who don't want kids to learn  evolution and even though the there have  been various ins and outs of the  creation and evolution controversy and  the creationists have always lost in  court this is an evolving targets  because creationism or shall we say in  response to the legal environment  there's a natural selection going on  here and we have different forms of  creationism and the current form of  creationism that we're coping with that  we're monitoring at NCSE today is  perhaps the most difficult to fight  because it doesn't appear to be the kind  of thing you can challenge directly in  court at any rate  get to that in a moment so let me tell  you about the pillars of creationism  because as a concerned citizen as a  scientist I hope you'd be able to  recognize them and understand the  refutations of the pillars so that you  can hopefully contribute to your own  community or state and help to improve  science education the first pillar is  the pillar which challenges the science  of evolution the claim is often made  that evolution is a theory in crisis  that scientists are giving up on  evolution that more and more scientists  are rejecting evolution as a failed  theory that will come as a surprise to  scientists but this is the claim that is  made and it's made very very loudly on  Talk radio's on blogs and letters to the  editor and so forth the second pillar of  creationism is an ideological pillar  it's the religious ideology that  evolution is incompatible with my  religious views therefore evolution  cannot be true and this takes various  forms the strongest form is if an  individual is a biblical literalist or a  qur'anic literalist this this point of  view is present and conservative  Christians conservative Muslims and  conservative choose the Middle Eastern  monotheism's if you will the argument is  made that my holy books when I look at  them I see special creation God created  everything especially in its present  form over a short period of time 6,000  years ago therefore evolution cannot be  true and indeed evolution is  incompatible with that particular  religious view some people reject  evolution because of a human  exceptionalism perspective that that  human beings are so superior to all  other organisms God must have created  especially there's a lot of ways that  that religion is used to oppose the  teaching of evolution the third pillar  is the fairness pillar the call it's a  cultural concept really it's a pillar  where the anti evolutionists are tapping  into some very deeply held american  cultural positions fairness balance free  speech  letting everybody speak you know town  hall meetings all that sort of thing and  and these are really great cultural  values there's nothing wrong with them  it's just that they are misapplied shall  we say when it comes to how we decide  what a kid's you learn in school now how  can you as a concerned citizen deal with  these three pillars as I say whatever  argument you hear you can probably fit  it into one of these three so if I can  just give you the generic refutations  then you can go from there there's  plenty of evidence for the particular  Zhaan various websites on NCS these  websites and others the evolution is  weak science pillar let's take a look at  that first well the best way to refute  that is to help your audience help the  person you're talking to understand that  in fact the consensus in the scientific  community supporting the idea that  evolution has happened the universe is  old etc is enormously strong there have  been many surveys of scientists there's  been a lot of survey research of  scientists showing upwards of the high  ninety percent of scientists accept the  idea of evolution it's hard to get  scientists to agree on whether it's  raining outside but there's this really  really strong acceptance consensus view  that evolution is valid science but the  public doesn't know this in the few  surveys that have been done where the  members of the public have been asked do  you think scientists agree that  evolution has happened the difference  between what the public thinks  scientists believe in what scientists  actually believe is like twenty thirty  percent it's really a big disconnect so  help the public understand that  evolution is valid science the second  pillar the religion pillar is best dealt  with by helping people understand that  there's a very wide diversity of opinion  within the Christian community I often  joke that I think more evolution is  taught in Catholic parochial schools  than in public schools because the  catholic church accepts something called  theistic evolution which is of course  evolution happened  but it was the gut way that God chose to  bring about the world according to his  plan there's varieties of theistic  evolution out there but it's basically  an idea it's a Christian theological  idea that is fully compatible with what  science shows us about living things  evolving through time Catholics  mainstream protestants official theology  is in fact theistic evolution so it is  not the case that if you are a Christian  you have to reject evolution some  Christian views absolutely but not all  Christian views most people think  there's a dichotomy on one side is  atheist evolution on the other side is  Christian creationism but that's false  that's just empirically wrong it's not a  dichotomy it's a continuum the third  pillar the fairness pillar is the most  difficult one to deal with because it is  so strongly rooted in American cultural  traditions the fairness pillar is best  dealt with by helping the public think  about this a little bit more more  carefully obviously we want debate in  our society about a lot of things people  should be free to express opinions  people should be free to argue  vehemently about one position or another  one opinion or another that's not really  what we're talking about what we're  really talking about is a much more  narrow issue we're really talking about  what do you teach in high school science  class because that is where these  policies that come to school boards or  the pressure that comes upon teachers  really come to what you should teach in  high school science class is no surprise  science and a high school science  teacher has the very important job of  picking from the huge array of subject  matter in say biology or physics or  chemistry or whatever that smaller range  of topics that is essential for young  learners who are just building an  understanding of that particular  scientific field in biology that one of  those core principles is evolution that  living things are related genetically in  this this huge genealogical tree of  living creatures through time  help the public understand that we're  not talking about inhibiting anybody's  free speech we're not talking about  science being dogmatic and and forcing  views upon students we're talking about  doing the same thing in high school  biology class as we do in physics or  chemistry or for that matter mathematics  and the English literature and geography  we teach the consensus view of the field  now if the creationists or the  intelligent design people or anybody  else who challenges evolution wants to  make the argument that they have a valid  science that deserves to be taught in  the public school classroom knock  yourself out but your job is to convince  the scientific community first what is  happening is that because of the  politicized nature of American education  curriculum decisions are made by elected  officials and elected officials are  looking over their shoulder two votes as  opposed to necessarily paying attention  to what's you know the best curriculum  because American education is  politicized there are pressures upon  teachers outside of science to change  the curriculum one way or the other they  should be protected from that students  should not be the the platform upon  which we fight the culture wars let the  creationists or the intelligent design  people or the anti evolutionists  demonstrate the scientific community  that they have valid views that  evolution is either a weak science or  that they've got a better alternative  and it'll trickle down to the high  school if it makes it into the consensus  they argue fairness frankly to teach  students that something is not valid  when there's such a strong scientific  consensus about it is the most unfair  thing you can do I would hope that being  familiar with the refutation zuv the  three pillars would give you more  confidence in standing up for good  science in your community if you hear of  a school board issue or some state  legislation or school board legislation  or if you even hear word that one of  your local teachers is reluctant to  teach evolution because  she or he feels that there's pressure  from the community I hope that you will  stand up for good science and the first  thing you can do is contact us at NCS  e-comm we can put you in touch with  people in your community who feel  likewise we can give you the ammunition  show we say the arguments that you can  use on the methods that you can use to  make a good argument to your local  decision makers because teachers need  help we're we get calls every week from  teachers who want some advice who want  some support to teach good science and  they deserve all the support that we as  scientists and concerned citizens can  give them thank you  you

